<Layouts.marketing current_path={assigns[:current_path]} flash={@flash} current_scope={@current_scope} page_title={@page_title}>
  <%!-- ───────────── HERO ─────────────
        Traffic arrives on "vibe coded website" having just read that their site
        looks like everyone else's, so the problem opens in that vocabulary. --%>
  <section class="bg-base-200 border-b border-base-300">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 lg:py-20">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <div class="badge badge-primary badge-outline gap-2 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-block w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-primary"></span>
          One afternoon · no design tools · no CSS
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl font-bold tracking-tight leading-[1.05]">
          Decide how it looks <span class="text-primary">once.</span>
        </h1>
      </div>

      <%!-- Heading, then the video, then the explanation and the CTAs. The
           video answers "what is this" faster than the paragraph does, so it
           goes above it; the paragraph is there for whoever does not press
           play.

           Full container width rather than a side column, which is a
           legibility constraint and not a taste one: the terminal is 72
           columns, and in the old 494px column that came to 6.6px per
           character against a floor of about 8. At 1152px it is 15.3px.

           Poster plus native controls, no click-to-swap placeholder — that
           resized the box mid-interaction and hid the play control behind a
           custom one. preload="none" keeps the page at 46KB until someone
           presses play; width and height stop it reflowing. --%>
      <figure class="mt-10">
        <div class="rounded-box overflow-hidden border border-base-300 shadow-sm bg-base-300">
          <video
            id="ds-hero-video"
            class="w-full aspect-video block"
            controls
            playsinline
            preload="none"
            width="1920"
            height="1080"
            poster="https://images.codemyspec.com/ds-hero-poster.jpg"
          >
            <source src="https://images.codemyspec.com/ds-hero.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
            Your browser cannot play this video.
            <.link href="https://youtu.be/baTqgRbRprM" class="link">Watch it on YouTube</.link>.
          </video>
        </div>
        <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/60 mt-3">
          One command, the five questions, and three real design systems from the same interview.
          1 min 40.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="max-w-3xl mt-10">
        <p class="text-lg text-base-content/70 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-base-content">
            Answer five questions about how your project should look. Every page built after that has
            to reference your answers instead of inventing its own.
          </strong>
          No design software, no CSS, and no arguing with a model about colors on every page.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4">
          <.link navigate={~p"/install"} class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">Install the plugin — free</.link>
          <.link navigate={~p"/product"} class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg">See the whole process →</.link>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- ───────────── THE PROBLEM ───────────── --%>
  <section class="border-b border-base-300">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <.eyebrow name="why they all look alike" />
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        Nobody told it how yours should look, so it used the average.
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-4 max-w-2xl space-y-4 text-lg text-base-content/70 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          <strong class="text-base-content">
            Vibe coded projects look alike because nothing said how yours should be different.
          </strong>
          The model reached for the average of everything it trained on. Your visitor has seen that
          average forty times, recognizes it before reading a word, and reads it as nobody having
          decided anything here.
        </p>
        <p>
          Prompting your way out does not hold. Art direction supplied one page at a time never
          carries to the next page, so six pages become six dialects of the same project.
        </p>
      </div>
      <%!-- Demoted out of the CTA slot on purpose. The reader's next step on a
           feature page is /install; these are context, so they read as quiet
           prose links rather than competing calls to action.
           Re-add /blog/vibe-coded-websites-look-the-same once it publishes. --%>
      <p class="mt-8 max-w-2xl text-sm text-base-content/60 leading-relaxed">
        The same thing happens
        <.link navigate={~p"/blog/prevent-slop-elixir-codebases"} class="link link-hover text-base-content/80">
          inside the code itself
        </.link>
        , not just on the surface. And if yours is already built and you would rather not redo it
        yourself,
        <.link navigate={~p"/vibe-coding-rebuild"} class="link link-hover text-base-content/80">
          it can be rebuilt for you
        </.link>
        .
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- ───────────── HOW IT WORKS ───────────── --%>
  <section class="border-b border-base-300 bg-base-200">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <.eyebrow name="how it works" />
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        An interview, then a file your project keeps.
      </h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-base-content/70 mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-base-content">
          Run <code class="text-base-content">/codemyspec:design ui</code> and answer the questions.
        </strong>
        There is nothing to configure first.
      </p>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 mt-12">
        <div class="card bg-base-100 border border-base-300 border-t-4 border-t-primary shadow-sm">
          <div class="card-body">
            <span class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-primary">
              While you answer
            </span>
            <h3 class="card-title mt-1">You watch it fill in.</h3>
            <p class="text-base-content/70">
              Your design system updates after every answer, so you can open it in a browser and see
              your palette and type as you decide. Change your mind while it is still cheap.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card bg-base-100 border border-base-300 border-t-4 border-t-secondary shadow-sm">
          <div class="card-body">
            <span class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-secondary">
              What you get
            </span>
            <h3 class="card-title mt-1">A page, not a document.</h3>
            <p class="text-base-content/70">
              One file in your project that documents your choices and renders them live at the same
              time. Show it to someone and they see the real thing, not a description of it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card bg-base-100 border border-base-300 border-t-4 border-t-accent shadow-sm">
          <div class="card-body">
            <span class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-accent">
              Afterwards
            </span>
            <h3 class="card-title mt-1">Every page has to cite it.</h3>
            <p class="text-base-content/70">
              From then on, each page states its layout and behavior before any code exists, and the
              agent is told to read your design system first rather than invent something.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-base-content/70 mt-10 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        <span class="font-semibold text-base-content">Roles, not swatches.</span>
        You assign colors to jobs in your product rather than picking values you like. That is what
        makes a palette survive contact with features you have not thought of yet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- ───────────── PROOF: three real systems ─────────────
        The strongest asset available. Same interview, three projects, nothing
        visually in common — and each choice justified by its own domain. This
        argues the case without copy. --%>
  <section class="border-b border-base-300">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <.eyebrow name="what comes out" />
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        Three projects. Three systems. One interview.
      </h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-base-content/70 mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-base-content">
          These answered the same five questions and share no palette, no density, and no type
          treatment.
        </strong>
        MetricFlow is an analytics product and wanted to feel alive. Keel is a ledger, and its file
        says why in one line: low eye-strain for long tax-season sessions.
      </p>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-5 mt-12">
        <figure :for={
          {slug, name, note} <- [
            {"metric-flow", "MetricFlow",
             "Custom dark theme, aurora gradient, indigo and cyan, panels with backdrop blur."},
            {"keel", "Keel",
             "Light Nord palette, system fonts with tabular figures, credit and debit as semantic colors."}
          ]
        }>
          <img
            src={"https://images.codemyspec.com/design-system-#{slug}.webp"}
            alt={"The #{name} design system"}
            class="w-full rounded-lg border border-base-300"
            width="1280"
            height="900"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/60 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-semibold text-base-content">{name}.</span> {note}
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <p class="text-base-content/70 mt-10 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        The subtler thing they prove is what did not happen: none of them look like CodeMySpec. The
        process does not impose a house style. It makes you decide, then holds every page you build
        afterward to the decision.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2 text-sm">
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/documentation/design-system-examples"}
          class="link link-primary font-medium"
        >
          All three, with the reasoning →
        </.link>
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/documentation/design-system-interview"}
          class="link link-primary font-medium"
        >
          The interview procedure in full →
        </.link>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- ───────────── HONEST LIMITS ─────────────
        Deliberately on the page. Everyone in this category overstates and the
        reader has been burned by it, so precision is the differentiator. --%>
  <section class="border-b border-base-300">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <.eyebrow name="what it does not do" />
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        What this doesn't do, said plainly.
      </h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-base-content/70 mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        You have probably been oversold once already, so here is the honest shape of it.
      </p>

      <ul class="mt-10 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
        <li :for={
          claim <- [
            "Your design system reaches the agent as something it must read, and as a required section on every page before code exists. What does not happen is a machine rendering your finished page and checking it against your design system pixel by pixel. No tool does that today.",
            "The check confirms your design decisions exist and are complete. It does not confirm the built page obeyed them. Reviewing pages is still yours, with much less to review.",
            "The instruction points the agent at your design system file, so the file has to exist. Skip the interview and it points at nothing.",
            "This step is optional. Nothing stops you building pages without it, you just inherit the average again."
          ]
        } class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="hero-minus-small-mini size-5 text-base-content/40 shrink-0 mt-0.5"></span>
          <span class="text-base-content/70 leading-relaxed">{claim}</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- ───────────── HOW IT FITS ───────────── --%>
  <section class="border-b border-base-300 bg-base-200">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <.eyebrow name="how it fits" />
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        One of several decisions you make once.
      </h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-base-content/70 mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-base-content">
          Design sits alongside the other things CodeMySpec has you settle before building: what the
          product does, how it is structured, what it runs on.
        </strong>
        The pattern is the same every time. You decide once through a structured interview, the
        decision lands in a file your project keeps, and later work has to reference it.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base-content/70 mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        That is what working a process instead of prompting means in practice. Design is the clearest
        instance of it, because the cost of not deciding shows up on the page where your customers can
        see it.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8">
        <.link navigate={~p"/product"} class="link link-primary font-medium">
          See the whole process →
        </.link>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- ───────────── FAQ ───────────── --%>
  <section class="border-b border-base-300">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <.eyebrow name="questions" />
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-3">Straight answers.</h2>
      <.faq faqs={@faqs} name="design-faq" class="mt-10 max-w-3xl" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- ───────────── CTA ───────────── --%>
  <section class="bg-base-200">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-20 lg:py-28 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        The cheapest afternoon in the project.
      </h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-base-content/70 mt-5 max-w-xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
        It is the one that stops you rebuilding pages later. Building with CodeMySpec is free.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4 justify-center">
        <.link navigate={~p"/install"} class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">Install the plugin — free</.link>
        <.link navigate={~p"/product"} class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg">See the whole process →</.link>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layouts.marketing>
